WEBVTT

NOTE
SYNTHETIC BENCHMARK FIXTURE. Fictional interview for the Verbatim eval set (Ledgerly study).
Participant p3: Priya Raman, Territory Sales Representative, Calloway Medical Supply (medical distribution), 300 employees. Not a real person.

00:00:04.651 --> 00:00:09.058
<v Interviewer>Hi Priya, thanks for jumping on. Is now still a good time?

00:00:09.454 --> 00:00:14.033
<v Priya Raman>Yes! Sorry, I'm in a parking lot outside a clinic.

00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:20.247
<v Priya Raman>I've got about twenty minutes before my next appointment, so the timing's actually perfect.

00:00:20.871 --> 00:00:27.032
<v Interviewer>Great. We'll record, if that's okay, and it's all about your experience with Ledgerly so far.

00:00:27.766 --> 00:00:29.446
<v Priya Raman>Sure, yeah, record away.

00:00:29.802 --> 00:00:32.558
<v Interviewer>Can you tell me what you do?

00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:42.345
<v Priya Raman>I'm a territory rep for Calloway Medical. We sell, um, wound care and surgical supplies, mostly to clinics and a few hospitals.

00:00:42.478 --> 00:00:52.249
<v Priya Raman>The company's about three hundred people. I cover the whole southern half of the state, so I basically live in my car.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:56.769
<v Interviewer>And how long have you been using Ledgerly?

00:00:58.006 --> 00:01:05.039
<v Priya Raman>Since the start of October, so like a month? Our finance team switched everyone over at once.

00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:14.338
<v Priya Raman>Before that we did expense reports in a spreadsheet and mailed paper receipts to the office. Like, physically mailed them, in an envelope.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.730
<v Interviewer>Wow. How has the switch felt?

00:01:18.836 --> 00:01:28.082
<v Priya Raman>Honestly, I love the app. I know that's probably not what you hear a lot, but for me it's been so much better.

00:01:28.335 --> 00:01:35.911
<v Priya Raman>I take a picture of the receipt, it pulls out the total and the restaurant name, I pick the client, done.

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:41.941
<v Priya Raman>It's like ten seconds. I used to spend my whole Sunday night on expense reports.

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:47.242
<v Interviewer>That's great to hear. What do you like about it specifically?

00:01:47.656 --> 00:01:51.232
<v Priya Raman>It's just... fast? It doesn't make me think.

00:01:51.425 --> 00:01:58.064
<v Priya Raman>The home screen shows my card, my recent stuff, and the big camera button. That's all I need.

00:01:58.304 --> 00:02:07.844
<v Priya Raman>And I can see when finance has looked at something, which, before, I had no idea until the reimbursement showed up three weeks later.

00:02:08.552 --> 00:02:12.562
<v Interviewer>How do you pick the client? Is that part easy?

00:02:13.853 --> 00:02:20.798
<v Priya Raman>Yeah, there's a list, and it remembers the last few I used, so it's usually right at the top.

00:02:21.032 --> 00:02:28.515
<v Priya Raman>Honestly, that little thing saves me so much. I see the same five or six accounts most weeks.

00:02:29.146 --> 00:02:33.286
<v Interviewer>Have there been any moments where it didn't work so well?

00:02:33.836 --> 00:02:42.293
<v Priya Raman>Oh, yeah. Okay, so the receipt thing is amazing when I've got signal. But a lot of where I go, I don't.

00:02:42.449 --> 00:02:52.428
<v Priya Raman>Some of these hospitals, the supply rooms are in the basement, and you've got, like, concrete everywhere. Or the rural clinics, there's just nothing out there.

00:02:53.562 --> 00:02:54.981
<v Interviewer>What happens then?

00:02:56.164 --> 00:03:04.096
<v Priya Raman>It says it's uploading, the little circle goes, and then — I don't know, it kind of gives up?

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:15.686
<v Priya Raman>Once I came back to the app that evening and there was a message that said "upload failed, try again," but the photo was gone, so I couldn't try again.

00:03:15.949 --> 00:03:21.255
<v Priya Raman>And I'd already tossed the receipt in the bin at the clinic, so.

00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:23.569
<v Interviewer>What did you do?

00:03:24.453 --> 00:03:33.214
<v Priya Raman>I emailed the restaurant and asked for a copy. Which, they were nice about it, but it's a little embarrassing.

00:03:33.331 --> 00:03:43.165
<v Priya Raman>Now I just take a regular photo with my phone camera first, as a backup, and then I do the app one when I'm back in town.

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:46.929
<v Priya Raman>Double work, but whatever, it's better than losing it.

00:03:48.168 --> 00:03:50.522
<v Interviewer>How often does it come up?

00:03:51.632 --> 00:03:56.389
<v Priya Raman>Maybe once or twice a week? It depends on the route.

00:03:56.641 --> 00:04:00.431
<v Priya Raman>Tuesdays are the bad day because that's my rural loop.

00:04:01.537 --> 00:04:05.385
<v Interviewer>Do you have a favorite clinic on your loop?

00:04:06.087 --> 00:04:14.191
<v Priya Raman>There's one in a tiny town with a bakery next door. I time my visits around the cinnamon rolls.

00:04:14.533 --> 00:04:16.169
<v Priya Raman>Don't tell my manager.

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:20.627
<v Interviewer>Can you walk me through a Tuesday, roughly?

00:04:21.443 --> 00:04:29.614
<v Priya Raman>Sure. I leave the house around six, coffee from the drive-through, and then it's three or four clinics before lunch.

00:04:29.739 --> 00:04:39.986
<v Priya Raman>Lunch is usually with a practice manager, because that's when they'll actually sit down with you. Then two more stops, and I'm home by six if I'm lucky.

00:04:40.299 --> 00:04:49.408
<v Priya Raman>So that's maybe five or six receipts on a Tuesday, and at least two of those are somewhere with no bars.

00:04:50.552 --> 00:04:53.541
<v Interviewer>Got it. Anything else that's been surprising?

00:04:54.217 --> 00:04:56.778
<v Priya Raman>The card limit, a little bit.

00:04:56.887 --> 00:05:08.562
<v Priya Raman>So I have a company card in the app, and I have a limit, and I thought I understood it, but then I got declined at a client dinner.

00:05:09.198 --> 00:05:10.253
<v Interviewer>Oh no.

00:05:11.193 --> 00:05:21.073
<v Priya Raman>Yeah, with the client sitting right there, the surgeon and his practice manager. So embarrassing. I paid with my own card and just smiled.

00:05:21.906 --> 00:05:24.773
<v Interviewer>What was going on with the limit?

00:05:25.831 --> 00:05:32.093
<v Priya Raman>So my limit's fifteen hundred a month. And I'd only spent like, I don't know, six hundred?

00:05:32.381 --> 00:05:44.154
<v Priya Raman>But I'd booked a hotel the week before, and the hotel put a hold on the card. And I guess the hold counted against my limit even though it wasn't a real charge.

00:05:44.301 --> 00:05:54.638
<v Priya Raman>And the app doesn't show holds. It just shows what's been charged. So I'm looking at nine hundred left and the card's saying no.

00:05:55.594 --> 00:05:58.112
<v Interviewer>How did you figure that out?

00:05:59.065 --> 00:06:06.016
<v Priya Raman>My manager asked finance, and finance asked Ledgerly. It took, like, a week to get an answer.

00:06:06.264 --> 00:06:09.255
<v Priya Raman>Nobody on our side understood it either.

00:06:09.908 --> 00:06:11.878
<v Interviewer>Has it happened since?

00:06:12.228 --> 00:06:19.395
<v Priya Raman>Once more, at a gas station, smaller amount. I've kind of stopped trusting the number on the screen.

00:06:19.677 --> 00:06:24.931
<v Priya Raman>I'll do mental math now, like, okay, minus the hotel, minus the rental car.

00:06:25.937 --> 00:06:31.412
<v Interviewer>You mentioned you like the app overall. Does the limit thing change that?

00:06:32.666 --> 00:06:35.038
<v Priya Raman>Not really? I mean it's annoying.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:42.069
<v Priya Raman>But the app itself is still my favorite thing finance has ever given us. Like, genuinely.

00:06:42.217 --> 00:06:47.100
<v Priya Raman>I tell the other reps, just use the app, don't fight it.

00:06:47.784 --> 00:06:51.796
<v Interviewer>How do you hear from finance when something's wrong?

00:06:52.659 --> 00:07:02.575
<v Priya Raman>Usually a little comment on the expense in the app, which I actually like. It's better than a reply-all email with my boss on it.

00:07:02.702 --> 00:07:09.625
<v Priya Raman>Our finance person, Carla, is lovely. She sends a thumbs up when things are good, which is weirdly motivating.

00:07:10.022 --> 00:07:15.107
<v Interviewer>[laughs] Does anything else in your work week compete with this for your attention?

00:07:16.304 --> 00:07:23.250
<v Priya Raman>Oh, everything. Product trainings, sample orders, the CRM, which I hate, I'll say that on the record.

00:07:23.428 --> 00:07:29.616
<v Priya Raman>Expenses used to be the worst part of my week. Now it's the CRM. So, progress.

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:32.269
<v Interviewer>What's wrong with the CRM?

00:07:33.266 --> 00:07:42.194
<v Priya Raman>It's a whole other interview. [laughs] Let's just say it was built by someone who has never been in a car.

00:07:43.433 --> 00:07:45.786
<v Interviewer>[laughs] That's quite an endorsement.

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:52.093
<v Priya Raman>I mean, compared to mailing envelopes? Anything would be. But no, it's actually nice.

00:07:53.079 --> 00:07:59.393
<v Interviewer>Little side question. You said you live in your car — how do you keep organized?

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.606
<v Priya Raman>Oh, I have a whole system. Tote bags. One tote per account.

00:08:05.882 --> 00:08:09.986
<v Priya Raman>My husband makes fun of me. And podcasts, lots of podcasts.

00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:14.254
<v Priya Raman>I'm on season four of this true crime one right now.

00:08:14.741 --> 00:08:15.786
<v Interviewer>Which one?

00:08:16.167 --> 00:08:21.266
<v Priya Raman>It's about a lighthouse keeper in Maine. Very slow. I love it.

00:08:22.478 --> 00:08:28.730
<v Interviewer>Ha. Okay, back to Ledgerly. Have the other reps adopted it the same way you have?

00:08:29.677 --> 00:08:38.911
<v Priya Raman>Some of them. The older reps, um, some of them still save everything up and do it at the end of the month in one go.

00:08:39.038 --> 00:08:47.785
<v Priya Raman>Which is fine, but then if it fails when they're out in a dead zone, they don't find out till way later.

00:08:48.832 --> 00:08:54.293
<v Interviewer>Is there anything about the rollout itself you'd change? The training, the timing?

00:08:55.530 --> 00:09:03.644
<v Priya Raman>Hmm. The training was a video, which was fine. Honestly I figured most of it out by just poking around.

00:09:03.777 --> 00:09:12.149
<v Priya Raman>Maybe tell people up front about the hotel thing. If I'd known that, I wouldn't have been surprised at dinner.

00:09:12.672 --> 00:09:15.248
<v Interviewer>Anything coming up that'll test it?

00:09:15.968 --> 00:09:22.982
<v Priya Raman>We have our national sales meeting in two weeks, in Phoenix. So, flights, hotel, a team dinner.

00:09:23.299 --> 00:09:32.447
<v Priya Raman>I'm a little nervous about the hotel hold again, to be honest. I might just ask finance to bump me for that week.

00:09:33.481 --> 00:09:38.979
<v Interviewer>If you could change one thing in the product, what would it be?

00:09:40.228 --> 00:09:47.325
<v Priya Raman>Two things. Keep my photo even if the upload fails, so I can just hit retry.

00:09:47.444 --> 00:09:55.437
<v Priya Raman>And show me the holds on my card, like, a pending line. Just tell me the real number.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.797
<v Interviewer>That's really clear. Anything else?

00:09:58.295 --> 00:10:06.285
<v Priya Raman>No, I think that's everything. Oh — my appointment's waving at me through the window. I should go.

00:10:07.077 --> 00:10:09.052
<v Interviewer>Perfect timing. Thank you, Priya!
